speechtext:
Mr. JEAN-FRANQOIS POULIOT (Temiscouata): Mr. Speaker, I desire to move the adjournment of the house for the following reasons:
Whereas, the Prime Minister of Canada, in his address to members of both houses of parliament, at Westminster, on May 11, 1944, declared:
Canada's war effort is a voluntary effort, . . . Our country has become increasingly proud of the fact that every fighting man from Canada serving across the seas, on the seas, and in the air is a volunteer. We can say, in very truth, that Canada's effort in this war is a voluntary effort.
Whereas, the Prime Minister of Canada, in an address delivered at Winnipeg on July 10, 1941, declared:
What has already been attempted in the way of reviving that issue (of conscription) at this time makes only too clear the advantage that might have been taken of a lengthy absence on my part from Canada, to force the issue into the arena of party strife.
How dire the consequences to our national unity this might have proved none can say.
Whereas, on April 27, 1944, two members of parliament asked for information on the orders of the day about the pressure to induce or coerce the personnel of various units to enlist for overseas service in the absence of the Prime Minister from Canada;
Whereas, the following day, a similar question was put by another member of parliament;
Whereas, in both instances the acting leader of the government did commit himself in his answer;
Whereas, according to a dispatch of the Canadian Press of April 27, 1944, Brigadier W. H. S. Macklin blamed the draftees who had not volunteered, and did not allow them to take part in a victory loan parade;
Whereas, the pressure and intimidation exercised by commanding officers at the Vernon, British Columbia, training centre to coerce the draftees to enlist for overseas service have been such that the city council of Quebec passed a resolution of protest, on May 12, in order that the voluntary policy expounded by the Prime Minister of Canada at Westminster should be carried on even during his absence from Canada;
I ask leave to move the adjournment of the house, under standing order 31, for the pur-
War Policy

It has contributed both directly and indirectly, I would say. According to the memorandum before me, the need for more graduate nurses and specialists has increased, so that it was decided to increase the grant to the Canadian Nurses' Association, as follows:
(a)	$25,000 for administration, to increase the campaign for recruitment of student nurses, and for increased publicity;
(b)	$155,000 for direct assistance to schools of nursing to provide for increased registration.

It is as follows:
Information has now been received in this office on the expenditure of the projects made possible through the grant given by the federal government to the Canadian Nurses' Association 1943-44, as the result of the most generous support afforded by you and others in your department. On behalf of the Canadian Nurses' Association, I have been requested to forward a report on these to you. In submitting their report regarding expenditure of the allocations made to them, much appreciation is expressed by the registered nurses' association in each province.
You will recall that the grant was made to support the following objectives:
_ (1) The development of war-time programmes, including extensions of facilities for giving postgraduate courses, in departments and schools .of nursing in universities, also in public health .organizations;
(2)	Recruitment of students and their ^preparation;
(3)	Direct assistance to schools of nursing to tpr.ovide for increased registration;
(4)	Administration, including the work of the national adviser and general publicity;
(5)	Bursaries to assist nurses in taking postgraduate work and qualifying as teachers, supervisors and administrators.
With the exception - of the allocations for administration and bursaries, expenditures of the grant have been made through the provincial associations of registered nurses, subject to the recommendations of the Canadian Nurses' Association and the approval of your department.
The following is a brief summary of a number of projects which have been undertaken in the provinces, and notations on the results as [DOT]reported by them.
1.	In seven provinces travelling instructors have been employed: to give refresher courses [DOT]to nurses in order to bring them up to date, when this is necessary; these courses are of .special value, as many married and retired nurses have been taking advantage of them and .are giving very valuable assistance during the present crisis; to advise with staffs in hospitals and boards of directors regarding problems arising out of the present crisis; to promote recruitment of students through direct contact with high schools. The assistance given by travelling instructors has been declared to be of the greatest value, and has been characterized in one province by hospital representatives as

one of the most effective measures ever taken to assist small hospitals and to bring expert advice and suggestions to them in a time of need.
